{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>You shall not have in your pouch a stone and a stone, a great and a small.</b> One must inquire: if the meaning is that the great weight is more than it should be and the small one is less than it should be, and both are false, and he weighs for himself with the great one and for others with the small one — if so, it is difficult. What is the meaning of what it says: ״אֶבֶן שְׁלֵמָה וָצֶדֶק יִהְיֶה לָךְ״? For the description 'whole' surely refers to the small one that is less than it should be, which needs to have its deficiency completed so that it be whole and not lacking — and why did it not say something corresponding to the great one? And perforce you must say that the word ״וָצֶדֶק״ was said concerning the false great one. Yet this is difficult: surely צֶדֶק is the opposite of both of them, so why do I need ״שְׁלֵמָה״? Rather, certainly this is its meaning: the great one is what measures properly, and the small one is the one deficient from what is proper. And what it said, ״אֶבֶן שְׁלֵמָה וָצֶדֶק״, all of it refers to the small one, for the Sages expounded the expression צֶדֶק (Bava Batra 88b): צַדֵּק מִשֶּׁלְּךָ וְתֵן לוֹ — 'give from your own and give to him.' Therefore it said, in the manner of 'not only this but also that': an entire and un deficient stone you shall have, that you not diminish your fellow at all — and not only that you not diminish him, but even ״וָצֶדֶ״, give from your own and give him more than what is coming to him. And if so, that the great one is the honest one, why did it say 'the great one shall not be in your pouch'? Why should the honest one not be in his pouch? And furthermore it is difficult: what is the meaning of what it said, ״כִּי תוֹעֲבַת ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ כָּל עֹשֵׂה אֵלֶּה כֹּל עֹשֵׂה עָוֶל״ — two expressions, why do I need both? And from the fact that it says ״כֹּל עֹשֵׂה עָוֶל,״ infer from it that ״כָּל עֹשֵׂה אֵלֶּה״ is not עָוֶל, and one must understand what this is." "The explanation of the matter is along the lines of what Solomon said: ״אֶבֶן וָאֶבֶן אֵיפָה וְאֵיפָה תּוֹעֲבַת ה׳ גַּם שְׁנֵיהֶם״ (Proverbs 20:10). If both are false, what is ״גַּם שְׁנֵיהֶם״ — from where would one come to distinguish between them? And what is the force of ״גַּם״? Rather, certainly it speaks of one that is false and one that is true, and it teaches that even the honest one is called an abomination, because the honest stone enables him to weigh with the false one. For if he did not have the honest stone, he would be afraid to weigh everyone with the false small one, for all the buyers, when they come home, would find their weight deficient and a public outcry would come against him in court, saying that he weighed us all with the small one. What can you say — perhaps it became deficient in your house? Did it become deficient for all of them in their houses? Such a thing is implausible. What does he do? He goes and weighs some of them with the deficient small one, and some of them with the honest great one. And if those people whom he weighed deficient come to court, he will argue in court: 'See, I sold to many people' — and he will ask those whom he weighed with the honest one, and they will testify that they are not deficient at all, and they will contradict the claimants who say he weighed deficient, and automatically the judge will reason for him and say that it became deficient at your place — for if not, how is one different from the others (Sanhedrin 65b), and why are these not deficient at all? And when the court sends for his weight, he will send the honest one. And so it turns out that were it not for the honest one, he could not sell to all of them with the small one. And so it is for this reason that he keeps the honest one — in order to validate his falsehood; therefore even the honest one is an abomination. And this is what Rashi explained: the great one that contradicts the small one, for through the great one he is able to disprove in court the deed of the small one. Therefore it said: ״כִּי תוֹעֲבַת ה׳ כָּל עֹשֵׂה אֵלֶּה״ — concerning what he weighed with the honest one, for this too is an abomination, since he does not weigh with the honest one except in order to deceive the world. ״כֹּל עֹשֵׂה עָוֶל״ — that is, the false one; but the first one is not עָוֶל, yet even so it is an abomination. And concerning this Solomon said: ״תּוֹעֲבַת ה׳ גַּם שְׁנֵיהֶם״ — to include the honest one as well." "And with this the juxtaposition is settled: ״זָכוֹר אֵת אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה לְךָ עֲמָלֵק״ is juxtaposed to the passage of weights. Rashi explained: if you lied in measures and weights, be afraid of the provocation of the enemy, as it is said (Proverbs 11:1), ״מֹאזְנֵי מִרְמָה תּוֹעֲבַת ה׳,״ and it is written immediately after (Proverbs 11:2), ״בָּא זָדוֹן וַיָּבֹא קָלוֹן וְאֶת צְנוּעִים חָכְמָה.״ And it is difficult: what is the connection between the provocation of the enemy and the weights? And furthermore: did Amalek come upon them because they lied in weights? Also regarding the proof it is difficult: why did it call them ״מֹאזְנֵי מִרְמָה״? It should have said ״מֹאזְנֵי שֶׁקֶר.״ And what is the קָלוֹן that it mentions? And how does the end of the verse, ״וְאֶת צְנוּעִים חָכְמָה,״ connect to its beginning? Rather, certainly it called the honest great stone ״מֹאזְנֵי מִרְמָה,״ for it is in order to deceive that he weighs with it, as above. And one who weighs with it is like one hiding his evil from people while making an eye upward as though he does not see. And such is the measure of the Holy One, blessed be He, in the manner that it is said (Proverbs 26:26): ״תִּכַּסֶּה שִׂנְאָה בְּמַשָּׁאוֹן תִּגָּלֶה רָעָתוֹ בְקָהָל.״ And in the manner that they said regarding the sotah (Sotah 9a): she acted in secret — the Holy One, blessed be He, reveals her disgrace; so too He said concerning these deceivers: you do oppression and robbery in secret — the Holy One, blessed be He, sends the enemy who will take all that is yours openly, and then retroactively your disgrace will be revealed, for all will know retroactively that you did oppression in secret and not with justice. This is what it said: ״בָּא זָדוֹן״ — this is the enemy who will come with זָדוֹן, brazenly and openly, and will come to light your קָלוֹן, your disgrace. And this is what is brought in the Yalkut in the name of the Mekhilta (Beshallah, parasha 1, 8): Rabbi Eliezer says: ״וַיָּבֹא עֲמָלֵק״ — he came brazenly, in the open. For all the calamities that came, came only by stealth; but this coming came brazenly, in the open — end of his words. And this is a great proof for our words: whatever Israel did in stealth, Amalek came openly in order to bring to light their disgrace. For the Holy One, blessed be He, does not execute judgment without justice, and when He sends the enemy to take what is yours, surely it was because in secret you did oppression and crookedness. And those who act thus think that they are wise in doing evil, just as in our generation they consider wise anyone who is sharp at deceiving — and Scripture says (Genesis 27:35): ״בָּא אָחִיךָ בְּמִרְמָה״ — and not 'with wisdom' — and took what is yours. However, one who fulfills ״וְהַצְנֵעַ לֶכֶת עִם אֱלֹהֶיךָ,״ and does no falsehood to his fellow even in private — he is the one called ״חָכְמָה,״ and this is what it said: ״וְאֶת צְנוּעִים חָכְמָה״ — and he is the opposite of the מֹאזְנֵי מִרְמָה that it mentioned." "And on account of this matter Amalek came, as it is said (Exodus 17:7–8): ״וְעַל נַסּוֹתָם אֶת ה׳ לֵאמֹר הֲיֵשׁ ה׳ בְּקִרְבֵּנוּ אִם אָיִן, וַיָּבֹא עֲמָלֵק.״ And the Yalkut concludes (in the name of the Mekhilta, Beshallah, parasha 6, 7): Rabbi Eliezer says: if He provides for us all our needs, we will serve Him, etc. From this we learn that they were not trusting in HaShem to give them all their needs, and most likely they were occupied with deceitful practices, so that they could not be made to disgorge in court, and they made an eye upward as though not seeing — as they wrote, ״הֲיֵשׁ ה׳ בְּקִרְבֵּנוּ אִם אָיִן״; therefore ״וַיָּבֹא עֲמָלֵק וַיִּלָּחֶם עִם יִשְׂרָאֵל בִּרְפִידִים,״ and they expounded it (ibid. parasha 1, 8) as the language of רִפְיוֹן יָדַיִם, slackening of hands — that is, that their judges' hands went slack, for they could not extract in court what they had wronged in secret. And so here it is said: ״וְאַתָּה עָיֵף וְיָגֵעַ וְלֹא יָרֵא אֱלֹהִים״ — Elohim literally, for a matter handed over to the heart is stated with ״וְיָרֵאתָ מֵאֱלֹהֶיךָ״; these are Elohim of judges, for one who has a great and small stone is not caught for his falsehood by judges. And therefore Amalek cut off the circumcision and flung it upward (Tanchuma 10), for the external circumcision — its purpose is to bring a person to the circumcision of the foreskin of the heart, and when their heart prepares deceit by burying his weights in salt, if so there is no benefit even in the external circumcision as long as they are foreskinned of heart. And further, according to the Sages (Tanchuma, parashat Tzav 14), the circumcision completes the name of Shaddai, and they spoke as if in doubt: ״הֲיֵשׁ ה׳ בְּקִרְבֵּנוּ אִם אָיִן״ — if so, there is no need for circumcision, and it is easy to understand. And they were not caught by the hand of Amalek except for 'the stragglers behind you,' whom the Cloud would expel because of their sin — for every deceitful person is banished from the precinct of the Shekhinah, as it is said (Psalms 5:5): ״לֹא יְגֻרְךָ רָע,״ and 'one who speaks lies shall not be established before His eyes' (Psalms 101:7), and all the more so one who does lies. And this is ״בִּרְפִידִים״ — their hands went slack, that is, they lacked strength, and they were ״עָיֵף וְיָגֵעַ,״ for they were not borne on eagles' wings like those within the Cloud, who are neither weary nor stumbling among them." "And by way of derash one may say: just as the passage ״זָכוֹר אֵת אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה לְךָ עֲמָלֵק״ is juxtaposed to the passage of weights, so too we read the passage of Zachor after the passage of Shekalim, which comes to atone for the sin of the Calf that was made from the weights of gold, because they were inflamed after money exceedingly, as explained above in parashat Ki Tissa (30:13), and all who are inflamed after it also falsify the weights. And for this sin Haman came upon Israel, from the seed of Amalek, with silver shekels, and Mordecai commanded that they not send forth their hands against the plunder, to repair this — and this is not the place to elaborate on it."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:14:37.611133720Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}